 (Set 8)"},{"number":19,"text":"Which method of heat transfer does not require a medium? (A. Conduction, B. Convection, C. Radiation)","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":20,"text":"The ------- law of thermodynamics is an application of conservation energy for heat and thermodynamic processes. (A. first, B. second, C. third)","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":21,"text":"In Which medium does the heat conduction take place? (A. in solid, B. in liquid, C. in gas)","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":22,"text":"An isobaric process occurs at constant -------. (A. volume, B. temperature, C. pressure)","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":23,"text":"An isothermal process occurs at a constant -------. (A. pressure B. volume C temperature)","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":24,"text":"He at conduction occurs most rapidly in -------. A. metals, B. wood, C. air","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":25,"text":"An ideal gas undergoes an isothermal expansion. What can be said about the change in its internal energy (∆U)? (A. ∆U > 0, B. ∆U = 0, C. ∆U < 0) SECTION B","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}]},"sectionB":{"B4b":[{"number":1,"text":"One liter of air is cooled at constant pressure until its volume is halved, and then it is allowed to expand isothermally back to its original volume. Draw the process on a p-V diagram. Find the change in internal energy of one mole of an ideal gas when its","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":2,"text":"-1 -1 temperature changes from 0℃ to 50℃. (R = 8.3143 J mol K )","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":3,"text":"Draw the diagram for thermodynamic cycle of heat engine. One gram of water (1 cm³) becomes 1671 cm³ of steam when boiled at a pressure of 1 atm. The heat of vaporization at this pressure is 2265 J g⁻¹. Compute the work done and the increase in internal energy. 5 (1 atm = 1.013 × 10 Pa) 3 3","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":4,"text":"One gram of water (1 cm ) becomes 1671 cm of steam when boiled at a pressure of 1 atm. The heat of vaporization at this pressure is 2265 J g⁻¹. Compute the work done and the increase in internal energy.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":5,"text":"Draw the diagram for thermodynamic cycle of heat engine.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":6,"text":"Draw a p-V diagram for the four reversible processes of Carnot cycle.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":7,"text":"Pl ot the pressure versus volume for isobaric and isochoric processes.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":8,"text":"If the rate of energy radiation from a blackbody of area 100 cm² is 42W, -8 -2 -4 find the temperature of that blackbody. (σ = 5.685 × 10 W m K )","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":9,"text":"Sketch the Pressure-Volume (p-V) diagram for an isothermal process of an ideal gas and label it appropriately.","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":10,"text":"How much heat per second is conducted through a wooden wall of area 25 m² and thickness 0.04 m if the temperature inside is 20 °C and the temperature outside is -10 °C? The thermal conductivity of wood is -2 -1 -1 8.37 × 10 W m K .","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":11,"text":"Draw the Pressure-Volume (p-V) diagram comparing the adiabatic process and the isothermal process for an ideal gas.","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":12,"text":"An automobile engine has an efficiency of 20% and produces an average of 23 000 J of mechanical work per second during operation. (i) How much heat input is required per second and (ii) how much heat is discharged as waste heat from this engine per second?","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":13,"text":"Draw a p-V diagram for the four reversible processes of Carnot cycle. An automobile engine has an efficiency of 20% and produces an average of 24 000 J of mechanical work per second during operation. (i) How much heat input is required per second? (ii) How much heat is discharged as waste heat from this engine per second?","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":14,"text":"In an isothermal process (27 ℃), 2 kilomole of an ideal gas is compressed from a volume of 4 litre to 1 litre. Find the work done on the system. -1 -1 (R = 8.3143 J mol K )","source":"2026 (Set 2)"}],"B5a":[{"number":1,"text":"Becomes hot. Explain how heat is transferred along the poker. Name the method of heat transfer in this case.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"}],"B7b":[{"number":1,"text":"The filament of a 100 W electric bulb is made of tungsten. The emissivity of tungsten is 0.3 and its length is 0.2 m. Find the diameter of the filament, if its temperature is 3000 K when the bulb is switched on. -8 -2 -4 (σ = 5.685 × 10 W m K )","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":2,"text":"(i ) How much heat per second is conducted through a wooden wall of area 25 m2 and thickness 0.04 m if the temperature inside is 20 ℃ and the temperature outside is - 10 ℃ ? The thermal conductivity of wood -2 -1 -1 is 8.37 × 10 W m K . (ii) An engine takes in 9220 J and does 1750 J of work each cycle while operating between 689℃ and 397℃ . What is its actual efficiency? In a room, the temperature of water in a kettle is 80 °C. The room","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":3,"text":"Temperature is 30 °C and the surface area of the kettle is 0.02 m². If the emissivity is 0.9, find the rate of heat loss due to radiation. -8 -2 -4 (σ = 5.685 × 10 W m K ) One gram of water (1 cm³) becomes 1671 cm³ of steam when boiled at a","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":4,"text":"Pressure of 1 atm. The heat of vaporization at this pressure is 2265 J g⁻¹. Compute the work done and the increase in internal energy. (l atm = 1.013 5 × 10 Pa)","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":5,"text":"In a room, the temperature of water in a kettle is 80 ℃. The room temperature is 30 ℃ and the surface area of the kettle is 0.02 m⁻². If the emissivity is 0.9, find the rate of heat loss due to radiation. (σ = 5.685 × -8 -2 -4 10 W m K )","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"B8b":[{"number":1,"text":"What is thermal conductivity? Why does heat transfer take place between an object and its surrounding? What is the relation between the total emissive power of an object and the total emissive power of a blackbody when both are at the same temperature?","source":"2025 (Set 8)"}]}}'::jsonb,0,true),
(12,'old_questions','Chapter 6','6','{"chapter":6,"sectionA":{"A1":[{"number":1,"text":"The decibel (dB) is the unit used for measuring sound intensity.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"An echo is repetition of sound due to the reflection of sound.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":3,"text":"Sound travels at different speeds depending on the medium through which it passes.","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":4,"text":"The intensity of an echo is less than that of the original sound.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":5,"text":"An echo is repetition of sound due to the vibration of sound.","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":6,"text":"Ultrasound cannot be heard by human ear.","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":7,"text":"An echo is repetition of sound due to the reflection of sound.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":8,"text":"Sound intensity is a direct measure of the energy reaching an ear drum.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":9,"text":"The speed of sound is higher in liquids than solids.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":10,"text":"The upper limit of audibility is about 200, 000 Hz.","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":11,"text":"The speed of sound is the same for different media.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":12,"text":"If the amplitude of the sound wave is doubled, the intensity becomes eight times greater.","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":13,"text":"Echoes can be heard separately from the original sound when the source of the sound is much closer to the observer than to the reflecting surface.","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":14,"text":"Reflection of sound does not obey the laws of reflection.","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":15,"text":"The intensity of a wave is inversely proportional to the square of the amplitude of the wave.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":16,"text":"The Doppler effect occurs only when the source of sound is moving, not the listener.","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":17,"text":"Sound waves can travel through a vacuum.","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":18,"text":"Doppler ultrasound is a technique that is used to examine blood flow.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":19,"text":"Soft materials absorb sound.","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"A2":[{"number":1,"text":"Sound travels ------- in moist (or) humid air.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"The ------- is the unit used for measuring sound intensity.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":3,"text":"The SI unit of sound intensity is the ------- per square metre.","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":4,"text":"The speed of sound is ------- in solids than liquids and gases.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":5,"text":"The ------- is the unit used for measuring sound intensity.","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":6,"text":"The ------- of the note is determined by its frequency.","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":7,"text":"For every 10 dB added to the sound level, the loudness of the sound is increased by -------.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":8,"text":"An echo is repetition of sound due to the ------- of sound.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":9,"text":"An ------- is repetition of sound due to the reflection of sound.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":10,"text":"The greater the frequency, the ------- is the pitch.","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":11,"text":"Ba ts and whales use ultrasound in -------.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":12,"text":"The loudness of the note depends upon the ------- of the wave that produces it.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":13,"text":"An ------- is repetition of sound due to the reflection of sound.","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":14,"text":"The range of frequencies a person can hear is known as the -------.","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":15,"text":"------- is the special ability of emitting sounds and interpreting the echoes.","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":16,"text":"The speed of sound is ------- in solids than liquids and gases.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":17,"text":"The number of vibrations per second, which determines the pitch of sound, is called the ------- of sound.","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":18,"text":"By using -------, the audible frequency range can be determined.","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":19,"text":"Young'' s modulus and the bulk modulus are ------- properties of the medium.","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":20,"text":"The sonar is a type of technology that works based on -------.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":21,"text":"The SI units of Young’s modulus and the bulk modulus are -------.","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":22,"text":"The pitch of a sound is determined by its --------.","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":23,"text":"Ba ts and dolphins use ------- in echolocation.","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"A3":[{"number":1,"text":"What material has the lowest speed of sound? (A. in solids, B. in liquids, C. in gases)","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":2,"text":"What does audibility refer to? (A. The quality of being able to be heard, B. The loudness of a sound, C. The frequency of a sound wave)","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":3,"text":"The intensity of a sound wave is directly proportional to the square of the ------- of the wave. (A. frequency, B. amplitude, C. wavelength)","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":4,"text":"Reflection of sound obeys the laws of -------. (A. reflection, B. refraction, C. emission)","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":5,"text":"The speed of sound is ------- in solids than liquids and gases. (A. higher, B. lower, C. the same)","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":6,"text":"What quantity determines the loudness of sound? (A. frequency, B. amplitude, C. wavelength)","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":7,"text":"The loudness of the note depends upon the ------- of the wave that produces it. (A. amplitude, B. frequency, C. wavelength)","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":8,"text":"Which of these effects describes the change in pitch we hear when a fast- moving motorbike goes past? (A. refraction, B. reflection, C. Doppler)","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":9,"text":"The ------- the amplitude, the louder the note. (A. same, B. lower, C. greater)","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":10,"text":"How does the intensity of a wave I vary with its amplitude A? 1 A. I ∝ A³, Β. 1 ∝ A², C. 1 ∝ $ A","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":11,"text":"If a star is approaching the earth the spectral lines would shift towards ------ end. A. blue, B. red, C. green","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":12,"text":"The speed of sound varies depending on: (A. The type of medium, B. The color of the medium, C. The shape of the sound wave)","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":13,"text":"For every 10 dB added to the sound level, the loudness of the sound is increased by ------- times. (A. 10, B. 20, C. 30)","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":14,"text":"What quantity that determines the loudness of sound? (A. frequency, B. amplitude, C. wavelength)","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":15,"text":"Which property of sound determines its loudness? (A. Frequency, B. Amplitude, C. Wavelength)","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":16,"text":"------- frequency sounds which are under the lower limit of audibility (20 Hz) are called infrasound. (A. Low, B. High, C. Normal)","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":17,"text":"What value of sound intensity in dB increases by a factor of 100? (A. 100 dB, B. 30 dB, C. 20 dB)","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":18,"text":"The Doppler effect is used to measure the ------- of detected objects. (A. velocity, B. length, C. distance)","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":19,"text":"The speed of sound is ------- in solids than liquids and gases. (A. lower B. higher C. smaller)","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":20,"text":"How does the intensity of a wave I vary with its amplitude A? A. I ∝ A2 B. I ∝ A3 C. I ∝ 1/A","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":21,"text":"When a source of sound moves away from a stationary observer, the observed frequency of the sound: (A. is greater than the source frequency, B. is less than the source frequency, C. is equal to the source frequency). SECTION B","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}]},"sectionB":{"B4b":[{"number":1,"text":"Doe s the photoelectric effect involve changes in frequency or wavelength of the waves? What specific effect is related to the change in frequency heard as a car, while sounding its horn, approaches and then moves away from an observer?","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":2,"text":"What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming ambulance -1 moving at 110 km hand emitting a steady 800 Hz sound from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":3,"text":"What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming ambulance -1 moving at 110 km hand emitting a steady 800 Hz sound from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹. What frequency does she receive after the ambulance has passed? (i) What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":4,"text":"Ambulance moving at 110 km h⁻¹ and emitting a steady 800 Hz sound from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹. (ii) What frequency does she receive after the ambulance has passed?","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":5,"text":"What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming ambulance -1 moving at 110 km hand emitting a steady 800 Hz sound from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹. What frequency does she receive after the ambulance has passed?","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":6,"text":"What value of sound intensity in dB increases by a factor of 10000?","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":7,"text":"Calculate the velocity of sound in air at STP. The density of air at STP is -3 1.29 kg m . Assume air to be diatomic with γ = 1.4. Also calculate the velocity of sound in air at 27 °C.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":8,"text":"A bat flying at a speed of 10 ms⁻¹ towards a stationary moth emits sound -1 wave of 340 ms . The bat hears the echoes 0.4 s later. (i) Calculate the distance travelled by the bat in 0.4 s. (ii) Calculate the distance travelled by the sound wave in 0.4 s.","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":9,"text":"Draw the waveforms of (i) two sounds with same loudness but different pitch and (ii) two sounds with same pitch but different loudness.","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":10,"text":"Calculate the velocity of sound in air at STP. The density of air at STP is -3 1.29 kg m . Assume air to be diatomic with γ = 1.4. Also calculate the velocity of sound in air at 27 ℃.","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"B7b":[{"number":1,"text":"What is the speed of sound in (i) a steel rod (ii) water? Young’s modulus 10 -2 3 -3 of steel is 20 × 10 Nm and density of steal is 7.8 × 10 kg m . The 10 -2 bulk modulus of water is 0.22 × 10 Nm and density of water is 3 -3 10 kg m .","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"(i ) What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming ambulance moving at 110 km h⁻¹ and emitting a steady 800 Hz from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹. (ii) What frequency does she receive after the ambulance has passed?","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":3,"text":"An ambulance travelling at 44 ms⁻¹ approaches a car heading in a same direction at the speed of 28 ms⁻¹. The ambulance driver has a siren sounding at 550 Hz. At what frequency does the driver of the car hear the siren? What will be the frequency of the siren heard by the driver of the car when the ambulance overtakes his car? (Speed of siren sound = -1 330 ms )","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":4,"text":"(i ) What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming ambulance moving at 110 km h⁻¹ and emitting a steady 800 Hz sound -1 from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms . (ii) What frequency does she receive after the ambulance has passed?","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":5,"text":"What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming ambulance moving at 110 km h⁻¹ and emitting a steady 800 Hz sound from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"}],"B8b":[{"number":1,"text":"What is Doppler effect? Express the Doppler equation in general form. Describe some applications of Doppler effect in a variety of fields.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":2,"text":"What is Doppler effect? A rhinoceros uses frequencies down to 10 Hz to talk one another. Can rhinoceros be able to communicate over long distances further than 4 km? Why? What is echo of a sound wave? Give its two applications. What will you do","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":3,"text":"If your classroom has echoes?","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":4,"text":"What is Doppler effect? Give three applications of Doppler effect. Give two applications of echo? Why is the intensity of an echo less than","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":5,"text":"That of the original sound? Is there a Doppler shift if the source and observer move in the same direction, with the same velocity? Explain. How many cases are there in Doppler effect? Describe them.","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":6,"text":"Express the general equation of Doppler effect. (i) What frequency is received by a person watching an oncoming","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":7,"text":"Ambulance moving at 110 km h⁻¹ and emitting a steady 800 Hz sound from its siren? The speed of sound on this day is 345 ms⁻¹. (ii) What frequency does she receive after the ambulance has passed?","source":"2026 (Set 1)"}]}}'::jsonb,0,true),
(12,'old_questions','Chapter 7','7','{"chapter":7,"sectionA":{"A1":[{"number":1,"text":"Two common defects of vision are farsightedness and nearsightedness.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"The pupil controls the amount of light energy entering the eye.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":3,"text":"The human eye is very similar to a camera in many aspects.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":4,"text":"The shutter speed is expressed in terms of the exposure time.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":5,"text":"The retina is the light sensitive area of the cells, at the front of the eye.","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":6,"text":"In the terrestrial telescope a convex lens is used as an eyepiece to produce an erect final image.","source":"2026 (Set 2)"}],"A2":[{"number":1,"text":"A camera is an optical instrument that forms and ------- an image of an object.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"An aperture is a circular opening through which the ------- passes.","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":3,"text":"Luminous flux (luminous power) Φ is measured in -------.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":4,"text":"Human eye is more sensitive to ------- light compared to other colors.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":5,"text":"A camera is an ------- instrument that records an image of an object.","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":6,"text":"The lens in a compound microscope nearer the object is called the -------.","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":7,"text":"The lens nearer the object is called the -------.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":8,"text":"The pupil automatically dilates in dim light and ------- in bright light.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":9,"text":"The fibre optics works on the principle of -------.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":10,"text":"One of the unique properties of a laser beam is -------.","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":11,"text":"The SI unit for illuminance is lux(lx). 1 lx is equal to -------- lm m².","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":12,"text":"The film consists of a thin layer of chemical compounds of -------.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":13,"text":"The ------- in the eye is similar to the aperture in the camera.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":14,"text":"In the terrestrial telescope a ------- lens is used as an eyepiece to produce an erect final image.","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":15,"text":"The ------- is the coloured portion of the eye and controls the amount of light entering the eye.","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":16,"text":"As the magnifying power increases, the ------- of images is reduced.","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":17,"text":"A camera is an ------- instrument that records an image of an object.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":18,"text":"The component of a camera that regulates the amount of light entering is known as the -------.","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":19,"text":"Illuminance Ev is the total ------- incident on a surface per unit area.","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":20,"text":"The ------- is a circular opening through which the light passes.","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":21,"text":"The aperture can be made wide or narrow by adjusting the -------.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":22,"text":"The human eye responds only to light having wavelengths between about 400 nm and -------.","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":23,"text":"The compound microscope has at least ------- convex lenses.","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":24,"text":"Pe ople with normal vision can see clearly objects which are at -------.","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"A3":[{"number":1,"text":"In an optical fibre, the light is transmitted through the -------. (A. core, B. cladding, C. jacket)","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"The core of fibre-optic cable is made of -------. (A. copper wire, B. thin strand of glass, C. lead wire)","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":3,"text":"Which type of lenses are used in telescope? (A. convex, B. mirror, C. concave)","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":4,"text":"Which part of the eye functions similarly to the diaphragm in a camera, controlling the amount of light that enters the eye? (A. Cornea, B. Lens, C. Iris)","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":5,"text":"------- is the defect of vision in which distant objects are seen clearly, but objects nearby are blurry. (A. Farsightedness, B. Nearsightedness, C. Blindness)","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":6,"text":"A microscope uses lenses to make ------- images of objects. (A. clear, B. reduced, C. enlarged)","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":7,"text":"A ------- controls the time during which light enters the camera through the lens. (A. film, B. aperture, C. shutter)","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":8,"text":"Pe ople with ------- can see clearly objects which are at infinity. (A. farsightedness, B. nearsightedness, C. normal vision)","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":9,"text":"What type of lens is used as an eyepiece to produce an erect final image in terrestrial telescope? (A. convex lens, B. concave lens, C. magnifying lens)","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":10,"text":"The yellow spot which is the most light-sensitive spot is -------. (A. behind the retina, B. in front of the retina, C. on the retina)","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":11,"text":"Which of the following does not make use of total internal reflection? (A. endoscope, B. oscilloscope, C. optical fibres)","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":12,"text":"What is the SI unit of illuminance? (A. candela per square metre (cd m²), B. lux (lx), C. candela (cd))","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":13,"text":"Which color of light is more sensitive to the human eye? A. red, B. blue, C. green","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":14,"text":"The core of a fibre-optic cable is made of -------. A. copper wire, B. thin strand of glass, C. lead wire","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":15,"text":"Which part of the human eye is responsible for adjusting the focus of light to form a clear image on the retina? (A. Iris, B. Lens, C. Cornea)","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":16,"text":"When taking a photograph in bright light a ------- f-number must be used. (A. small, B. large, C. zero)","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":17,"text":"A microscope uses lenses to make ------- images of objects. (A. clear, B. reduced, C. enlarged)","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":18,"text":"What principle is utilized in a compound microscope to achieve high magnification? (A. Refraction of light through a convex lens, B. Total internal reflection, C. Diffraction of light)","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":19,"text":"When near objects are viewed, the ciliary muscles contract and the eyelens becomes -------. (A. less convex, B. more convex, C. less concave)","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":20,"text":"Which of the following is the SI basic unit of luminous intensity? (A. lux (lx), B. candela (cd), C. lumen (lm))","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":21,"text":"The film consists of a thin layer of chemical compounds of -------. (A. copper, B. silver, C. gold)","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":22,"text":"------- is a defect of vision in which near objects are seen clearly, but objects far away are blurry. (A. Farsightedness B. Nearsightedness C. Blindness)","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":23,"text":"Which wavelength corresponds to the maximum sensitivity of the human eye under daylight? A. 450 nm (blue), B. 555 nm (green), C. 700 nm (red)","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":24,"text":"What is the main function of the cladding in an optical fibre? (A. To protect the core from external damage, B. To increase the core''s refractive index, C. To provide a lower refractive index to cause total internal reflection in the core) SECTION B","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}]},"sectionB":{"B4b":[{"number":1,"text":"Draw ray diagram of an astronomical telescope.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"A compound microscope consists of two thin lenses, an objective of focal length 20 mm and an eyepiece of focal length 50 mm, placed 220 mm apart. If the final image is viewed at infinity, calculate the distance of the object from the objective.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":3,"text":"Draw the optical features of the eye and label each part.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":4,"text":"Draw the optical features of the eye.","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":5,"text":"Draw the optical features of the human eye with labeled diagram.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":6,"text":"Draw the diagram of a terrestrial telescope that uses a concave lens as an eyepiece.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":7,"text":"Draw the illustration of four quantities of photometry.","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":8,"text":"Draw the diagrams of human eye showing how the eyelens changes its shape to obtain a sharp image on the retina when viewing (i) near objects and (ii) distant objects.","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":9,"text":"Draw a ray diagram of a terrestrial telescope that used a concave lens as an eyepiece.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":10,"text":"Draw a ray diagram of a terrestrial telescope.","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":11,"text":"Draw a clear diagram of the human eye and label its important parts.","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":12,"text":"Draw the ray diagram of an astronomical telescope. A telescope consists of two converging lenses. When in normal adjustment, so that the image of a distant object is formed at infinity, the lenses are 500 mm apart, and the magnifying power is 9. What are the focal lengths of the two lenses?","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":13,"text":"Draw a ray diagram of an astronomical telescope.","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"B5a":[{"number":1,"text":"How many photons are emitted in 1 s by 7.5 mW of CO2 laser with the -34 wavelength of 10.6 μm? (Planck’s constant, h = 6.62 × 10 Js, 8 -1 c = 3 × 10 ms )","source":"2024 (Set 6)"}],"B7b":[{"number":1,"text":"In a laser unit, laser beam of wavelength 6328 Å is emitted. How many photons are released per second if the output power is 1 mW? (h = 6.625 × 10!\"# Js, c = 3 × 10$ ms⁻¹)","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":2,"text":"A compound microscope consists of two thin lenses, an objective of focal length 20 mm and an eyepiece of focal length 50 mm, placed 220 mm apart. If the final image is viewed at infinity, calculate the distance of the object from the objective.","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":3,"text":"A telescope consists of two thin converging lenses of focal lengths 100 cm and 10 cm respectively. It is used to view an object 2000 cm from the objective. What is the separation of the lenses if the final image is 25 cm from the eyelens? Determine the magnifying power for an observer whose eye is close to the eyelens.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":4,"text":"The luminous intensity of a source is 200 cd. The mounting height of the lamp is 6 m from the ground. Find the illuminance on the ground Ev: (i) just below the lamp, (ii) 3 m horizontally away from the lamp on the ground.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":5,"text":"How many photons are emitted per second by a 7.5 mW of CO2 laser with the wavelength of 10.6 µm? (h = 6.62 × 10-34 Js)","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":6,"text":"Two light sources of 8 cd and 12 cd are placed on the same side of the photometer screen at a distance of 40 cm from it. Where should an 80 cd source be placed to balance the illuminance?","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":7,"text":"Light from a lamp is falling normally on a surface distant 10 m from the lamp and the illuminance on it is 10 lux. In order to increase the illuminance 9 times, what distance will the lamp be placed from the surface?","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":8,"text":"An astronomical telescope has its two-lens space 75.2 cm apart. If the objective lens has a focal length of 70 cm, what is the magnifying power of this telescope when viewing with a relaxed eye?","source":"2026 (Set 2)"}],"B8b":[{"number":1,"text":"What are meant by farsightedness and nearsightedness and how can they be corrected?","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":2,"text":"What is a useful application of fibre optics? Name the dispersion elements used in the optical spectrometer. State two essential processes to initiate a laser action. What are meant by farsightedness and nearsightedness and how can they be","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":3,"text":"Corrected?","source":"2026 (Set 1)"}]}}'::jsonb,0,true),
(12,'old_questions','Chapter 8','8','{"chapter":8,"sectionA":{"A1":[{"number":1,"text":"Acompact disc is an example of reflection gratings.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":2,"text":"The position of fringes depends on the wavelength of the light source.","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":3,"text":"The width of the central maximum depends on width of the slit.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":4,"text":"Constructive interference occurs when the phase difference is π radians.","source":"2025 (Set 10)"}],"A2":[{"number":1,"text":"Monochromatic light means that it has a ------- wavelength.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"The angular position of a point of a periodic wave is known as the -------.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":3,"text":"The angular position of a periodic wave is known as the ------- angle.","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":4,"text":"Wavefront is always perpendicular to the direction of -------.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":5,"text":"A Wavefront is a locus of all points having the same ------- in the wave.","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":6,"text":"Bright fringe has ------- intensity.","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":7,"text":"There are two types of wave front; plane-wave front and ------- wave front.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":8,"text":"The angular position of a point of a periodic wave is known as the -------.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":9,"text":"The angular position of a point of a periodic wave is the -------.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":10,"text":"Bright and dark fringes are formed -------.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":11,"text":"------- light means that it has a single wavelength.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":12,"text":"There are two types of wavefront; plane wavefront and -------.","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":13,"text":"When light passes through a small opening or around an obstacle, it spreads out, a phenomenon known as -------.","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":14,"text":"The distance between two adjacent bright (or dark) fringes is called -------.","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":15,"text":"There are two types of wavefront: plane wavefront and ------- wavefront.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":16,"text":"An optical device used to separate light into its component wavelengths by diffraction is called ------- grating.","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":17,"text":"A ------- can disperse light into its wavelength components.","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":18,"text":"------- is always perpendicular to the direction of propagation (or) rays.","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":19,"text":"Distance between two ------- bright (or dark) fringes is called the fringe width.","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":20,"text":"------- coherent light is used in the double slits experiment.","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":21,"text":"------- light is used in Young’s double-slit experiment.","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":22,"text":"A diffraction grating consists of a large number of parallel slits of the same width separated by equal ------- spaces.","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}],"A3":[{"number":1,"text":"Wavefront is always ------- to the direction of propagation (or) rays. (A. perpendicular, B. opposite, C. parallel)","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":2,"text":"What does monochromatic light refer to? (A. Light of various wavelengths, B. Light of a single wavelength or colour, C. Light with no polarization)","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":3,"text":"Monochromatic light has a single -------. (A. wavelength, B. amplitude, C. intensity)","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":4,"text":"Cons tructive interference has ------- intensity. (A. minimum, B. maximum, C. normal)","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":5,"text":"If the path difference is ------- the waves will interfere destructively. (A. λ/2, B. λ, C. 2λ)","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":6,"text":"------- needs at least two coherent sources of light. (A. Diffraction, B. Refraction, C. Interference)","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":7,"text":"What type of wave emerges from a slit when a plane wave passes through a slit? (A. plane wave, B. circular wave, C. both circular and plane wave)","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":8,"text":"Huyge ns'' principle states that every point on a ------- may be considered as a source of secondary wavelets. (A. wavelet, B. wavefront, C. new wavefront)","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":9,"text":"Which colour of light will have the smallest diffraction angle in a grating? (A. red, B. orange, C. violet)","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":10,"text":"Cons tructive interference has ------- intensity. (A. minimum, B. maximum, C. normal)","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":11,"text":"If the violet light in Young''s double slit experiment is replaced by red light, the fringe width will -------. A. decrease, B. increase, C. not change","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":12,"text":"The angular position of a point of a periodic wave is called the -------. A. phase angle, B. wavelength, C. amplitude","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":13,"text":"What is the phase difference between two waves that are perfectly in phase? (A. 0°, В. 90°, С. 180°)","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":14,"text":"Two positions are in phase if phase difference are -------. (Α. 0, 2π, 4π, 6π …., Β. π, 3π, 5π …., C. π/2, 3π /2, 5π /2….)","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":15,"text":"If The path difference is ------- the waves will interfere destructively. (Α. λ/2, Β. λ, C. 2λ)","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":16,"text":"When two waves are completely out of phase, what is their phase difference? (A. 0°, B. 90°, С. 180°)","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":17,"text":"Monochromatic light means that it has a ------- wavelength. (A. single, B. double, C. triple)","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":18,"text":"In a double slit experiment, if the distance between the slits and the screen is increased, the fringe width will -------. (A. increase, B. decrease, C. not change)","source":"2025 (Set 11)"},{"number":19,"text":"Monochromatic light means that it has a ------- wavelength. (A. single, B. double, C. multiple)","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":20,"text":"The bright and dark fringes in a diffraction pattern are created by ------- of diffracted light waves. (A. interference B. reflection C. refraction)","source":"2026 (Set 1)"},{"number":21,"text":"Slits per unit length in a diffraction grating is called -------. A. grating constant, B. grating element, C. grating frequency","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":22,"text":"What is the nature of the wavefront far away from the source? (A. Spherical wavefront, B. Plane wavefront, C. Cylindrical wavefront) SECTION B","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}]},"sectionB":{"B4b":[{"number":1,"text":"A beam of green light from a laser light source is diffracted by a slit of width 0.55 mm. The diffraction pattern forms on a wall 20.6 m beyond the slit. The distance between the positions of the first minima on both sides of the central bright fringe is 4.1mm. Calculate the wavelength of the laser light.","source":"2024 (SQ)"},{"number":2,"text":"What is the phase difference for two waves of the same frequency arriving at a point with the respective path lengths of 6 λ and 4.5 λ? Is it constructive or destructive interference?","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":3,"text":"Light of wavelength 580 nm is incident on a slit of width 0.3 mm. The observing screen is placed 2 m from the slit. Find the positions of the first dark fringes and the width of the central bright fringes.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":4,"text":"A grating containing 4000 slits per centimetre is illuminated with a monochromatic light and produces the second-order bright line at a 30° angle. What is the wavelength of the light used?","source":"2025 (Set 11)"}],"B5a":[{"number":1,"text":"Second-order maximum be for 625 nm wavelength green light? How many lines per centimetre are there in a diffraction grating that gives a","source":"2025 (Set 12)"},{"number":2,"text":"First-order maximum for 470 nm blue light at an angle of 25°?","source":"2026 (Set 1)"}],"B7b":[{"number":1,"text":"Distance of 0.75 mm. A screen is placed 1.50 m away to capture the st interference pattern. What is the distance from the 1 order maximum to the 3rd order minimum? If the distance between two slits is 0.05 mm and the distance to a screen is","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":2,"text":"2.5 m, find the spacing between the first and second-order bright fringes for yellow light of wavelength 580 nm. Light of wavelength 580 nm is incident on a slit of width 0.3 mm. The","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":3,"text":"Observing screen is placed 2 m from the slit. Find the positions of the first dark fringes and the width of the central bright fringes. A grating containing 4000 slits per centimetre is illuminated with a","source":"2025 (Set 10)"},{"number":4,"text":"Monochromatic light and produces the second-order bright line at a 31° angle. What is the wavelength of the light? And what is the colour of the light used?","source":"2025 (Set 8)"}],"B8b":[{"number":1,"text":"In the context of light?","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":2,"text":"What are the conditions for constructive interference and destructive interference?","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":3,"text":"What are the differences between interference and diffraction? What will happen on the interference pattern of a double slit, if","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":4,"text":"(i) separation between the two slits is decreased? (ii) wavelength of light is increased?","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":5,"text":"What are the conditions for constructive interference and destructive interference? What will happen on the interference pattern of a double slit if, (i) separation between the two slits is decreased? (ii) wavelength of light increased? What is the difference between diffraction grating and prism? What is an","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":6,"text":"Example of diffraction grating in everyday life? How are diffraction gratings used in spectrometers? What are the conditions for constructive interference and destructive","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":7,"text":"Interference? What is the difference between Fresnel diffraction and Fraunhofer diffraction? Why do two light waves sometimes combine to make a brighter light and","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":8,"text":"Other times cancel each other out? Illustrate the position of particular bright and dark fringes on the screen in","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":9,"text":"Young''s double slit experiment. What happens when two light waves meet, and why do they sometimes","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":10,"text":"Make light brighter or cancel it out? What are the conditions for constructive interference and destructive","source":"2025 (Set 9)"},{"number":11,"text":"Interference between two coherence waves? What does \"two waves are coherent\" mean? Why is monochromatic","source":"2026 (Set 2)"},{"number":12,"text":"Coherent light used in the double slits experiment?","source":"2026 (Set 3)"}]}}'::jsonb,0,true),
(12,'old_questions','Chapter 9','9','{"chapter":9,"sectionA":{"A1":[{"number":1,"text":"For a vacuum, εᵣ = 1 and for other insulating materials εᵣ > 1.","source":"2024 (Set 1)"},{"number":2,"text":"When one side of a capacitor holds a positive charge, the other side holds an equal negative charge.","source":"2024 (Set 2)"},{"number":3,"text":"Variable capacitor is used as tuning capacitor.","source":"2024 (Set 3)"},{"number":4,"text":"A capacitor is an electrical device that stores electrical energy in the form of a magnetic field.","source":"2024 (Set 4)"},{"number":5,"text":"Variable capacitors mostly use air as the dielectric medium.","source":"2024 (Set 5)"},{"number":6,"text":"The capacitors can be used in both electrical and electronic applications.","source":"2024 (Set 6)"},{"number":7,"text":"A capacitor stores electrical energy in the form of a magnetic field.","source":"2024 (Set 7)"},{"number":8,"text":"If one conductor of a capacitor has a charge +Q the other has a −Q.","source":"2025 (Set 1)"},{"number":9,"text":"The dielectric strength of insulating materials is smaller than that of air.","source":"2025 (Set 2)"},{"number":10,"text":"A supercapacitor (SC) is also called an ultracapacitor.","source":"2025 (Set 3)"},{"number":11,"text":"The capacitance of a capacitor depends on the nature of the insulator between the two conductors of that capacitor.","source":"2025 (Set 4)"},{"number":12,"text":"A capacitor stores electrical energy in the form of magnetic field.","source":"2025 (Set 5)"},{"number":13,"text":"The total capacitance of capacitors in parallel is equal to the sum of their individual capacitances.","source":"2025 (Set 6)"},{"number":14,"text":"Fi lm capacitors and ceramic capacitors have positive and negative terminals.","source":"2025 (Set 7)"},{"number":15,"text":"Variable capacitors mostly use air as the dielectric medium.","source":"2025 (Set 8)"},{"number":16,"text":"Capacitors in parallel have the same charge on each capacitor.","source